   FC2-c064.back: 'Every strike, not only the at-the-money one: C − P = S − e^{−rτ}K for every strike K > 0 (Theorem 1.2). The proof uses the four payoffs, A1 and A2 (no income, free short sales and lending), A3, and the rate of A4. It uses no distribution for S_τ (Remark 1.3).' -> 'Every strike, not only the at-the-money one: C − P = S − e^{−rτ}K for every strike K > 0 (Theorem 1.2). The proof uses the four payoffs, A1, A2, A3 and A5 (no income, free short sales and lending, no arbitrage, and European exercise at expiry), and the rate of A4. It uses no distribution for S_τ (Remark 1.3).'
   FC2-c072.back: 'The parity cost is zero at each of the five strikes, 80 to 120 USD. None of the 40 (strike, expiry spot) pairs has a nonzero parity payoff (Lab 1).' -> 'Zero at each of the five strikes, 80 to 120 USD (Lab 1).'
   FC2-c072.front: 'On the synthetic price set of Lab 1, what is the parity cost at each strike, and how many (strike, expiry spot) pairs have a nonzero parity payoff?' -> 'On the synthetic price set of Lab 1, what is the parity cost at each strike?'
   FC2-c078.back: 'No. Under A1 to A5 and (N), C ≤ S (Theorem 2.2(a)). Selling the call and buying the share has initial value S − C < 0 and payoff min(S_τ, K) ≥ 0 (uses (N)), which A3 excludes.' -> 'No. Under A1 to A5 and (N), C ≤ S (Theorem 2.2(a)). If C were above S, selling the call and buying the share would have initial value S − C < 0 and payoff min(S_τ, K) ≥ 0 (uses (N)), which A3 excludes.'
   FC2-c093.back: 'V(n, k), the backward-induction value of Definition 3.2 (Theorem 3.3).' -> 'Yes. The price at node (n, k) is V(n, k), the backward-induction value of Definition 3.2 (Theorem 3.3).'
   FC2-c093.front: 'Under A1, A2, A3, A5, A6 and A6′, what is the price at node (n, k) of the multi-period tree?' -> 'Under A1, A2, A3, A5, A6 and A6′, is the arbitrage-free price at each node of the multi-period tree given by backward induction?'
